diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce the reported outage. Layer-2 and local L3 in Office A are healthy. From PC1 (10.1.0.100) I can ping PC2 (10.1.0.101) with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs, and vice‑versa. PC1 also pings the default gateway 10.1.0.1 successfully. PC1’s ARP table resolves 10.1.0.1 to MAC 0e:35:24:b4:1b:9d, which matches DSWA1’s bridge MAC, confirming proper first-hop connectivity.

Distribution switch DSWA1 is up: VLAN 10 SVI br0.10 is UP and carries both 10.1.0.2/24 and the virtual 10.1.0.1/32; inter-switch aggregation (eth3/eth4) is UP; core uplinks (eth5/eth6) are UP. Access switches are also up: ASWA2 (PC1’s access) and ASWA3 (PC2’s access) show their client and uplink interfaces UP and bridging normally. Cross-subnet reachability (PC1/PC2 to 10.3.0.100) also works, indicating the distribution and core are forwarding correctly.

Mechanism: If DSWA1 or an access link had failed, ARP for 10.1.0.1 would not resolve and local pings would fail. Instead, ARP and ICMP both succeed, so the fabric is operating normally.

Localization: No faults found on DSWA1 (br0/br0.10), ASWA2 (eth0 uplink eth1/eth2), or ASWA3.

Root cause: No active network fault; the issue is not reproducible at this time.

Corrective fix: No restart required. Ask the user to retest. If the symptom recurs, capture a timestamp and check for intermittent cabling/NIC issues on the PC or its access port (ASWA2 eth0), but the network currently operates normally. 
=== END ===